What exactly is a fixture?
A pre-built resource/environment that Playwright provides to your test.
Playwright Test
       │
       ├── page
       ├── context
       ├── browser
       ├── browserName
       └── request


await page.goto(...);
await page.getByPlaceholder('Username').fill('Admin');
await page.getByPlaceholder('Password').fill('admin123');
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Login' }).click();
await expect(page.getByText('Dashboard')).toBeVisible();
await expect(page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Dashboard' })).toBeVisible();

What is a fixture in Playwright?
A fixture is a reusable test resource or environment provided by Playwright Test. 
Playwright has built-in fixtures such as page, context, browser and request. 
For example, the page fixture provides an isolated Page instance to the test. 
We can also create custom fixtures using test.extend() for framework-specific requirements.

What is strict mode violation in Playwright?
Playwright locators are strict when an action or assertion expects a single element. 
If the locator matches multiple elements, Playwright throws a strict mode violation. 
I would normally make the locator more specific using role, accessible name, attributes, 
filtering, or a better locator strategy rather than blindly using first or nth.

